 about ending austerity i mean are you  worried about this pitch from the prime  minister  well it looks as though she's making a  pitch for labor support because she's  got  so little support in her own party if  you read the stories this morning  they're a group of conservative mps who  are threatening to well have a coup  and then not vote for her own budget  they're falling apart  so it's no wonder she's desperately  looking for support fairly cynically  from  anywhere no i'm not worried at all look  this issue she announced during her  speech at conference about end of  austerity they've announced it three  times already and it's not happened  and isn't ironic that in the very week  that she announces the end of austerity  we discover that members of her cabinet  are being briefed  that families will lose out on 200  pounds a month as a result of the  rollout of universal  universal credit and what the institute  of fiscal studies is saying  75 of the welfare cuts have still got to  be rolled out over the coming couple of  years so  there's no end of austerity quite the  reverse what's happening is the  austerity rolls on  and people are really suffering some of  the most vulnerable within our society  okay i want to talk to you about  universal credit because  labour's current policy on universal  credit which you're very  um openly open in your criticism of it  your current policy is just to pause it  and to fix it so  can you not give us a clear answer now  about whether or not it would be  labour's policy you know in the next  election or if the labour government  would actually scrap universal credit or  if you just keep it despite your  criticisms  i think most people now are coming to  the conclusion it's got to be scrapped  i've been listening to people of the  last few weeks about the role out in  their particular areas i've been looking  at what the government has said that  how they're seeking to reform it the  reforms haven't worked  i think we're at that stage now that  it's not sustainable anymore it's not a  it's not a system that it can work it's  not a system that's providing the  safety's net that people expect it when  they're well they need support  so i don't think i think we're moving to  a position now where it's just not  sustainable it'll have to go  if there were reforms that that we could  be effective  uh fair enough let's reform but we we've  looked and looked and we can't see that  either government proposals  or other proposals could reform it the  system is so  it's in shambles and actually i think  it's actually iniquitous as well  to have a government ministers being  privately briefed that families are  going to lose  200 pounds a month and these are some of  the poorest families  in our community that's just not  acceptable so i i think we're moving  towards a conclusion now that it's  you can't save the thing it's got to go  so if  if that is the case and you say that  it's got to go i mean the principles of  universal credit are hard to  disagree with which is that it should  pay to be in work rather than to not be  in work  and also that the system should be um  simplified i mean 1.9 billion pounds has  been  plowed into universal credit what are  you going to replace it with i mean it's  very easy to point the finger  and quickly well that's what we want to  see but what would you replace it with  well that's exactly what we've been  saying over the last well last year and  since the last general election we now  need a proper consultation  a root and branch examination of how we  can go forward to make sure people do  get the proper support  the principles are bringing benefits  together in one so it's a much simpler  system we all support  but this hasn't done that it's made it  more complex but in addition to that  it seems to have actually penalized the  very people we want to support as i say  we've got four million children out  there in our community  two-thirds of them are i